 ## Overview
-Flash Cache is a volumetric inverse rendering method that reduces bias and variance in radiance estimation by using a high-capacity Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) as a control variate for incoming illumination [S1]. Unlike prior radiance caches that use low-capacity models (introducing bias via inaccurate reflection estimation) or render NeRFs at single evaluation points (introducing bias via volume rendering integral approximation), Flash Cache provides an unbiased, low-variance estimate of the rendering equation [S1]. It achieves this by replacing the recursive incoming radiance term with a NeRF-based cache output, evaluated via importance sampling, while maintaining computational tractability through a "Fast Cache" approximation [S1].
+Flash Cache is a volumetric inverse rendering method that reduces bias and variance in radiance estimation by using a high-capacity Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) as a control variate for incoming illumination [S1]. Unlike prior radiance caches that use low-capacity models, which fail to capture high-frequency and near-field illumination, Flash Cache uses a high-capacity cache [S1]. Unlike methods that use a NeRF as a cache but render at only a single evaluation point, which causes bias, Flash Cache uses the cache as a control variate for unbiased estimation [S1]. It achieves this by replacing the recursive incoming radiance term with a NeRF-based cache output, evaluated via importance sampling, while maintaining computational tractability through a "Fast Cache" approximation [S1].
 
